Add a microphone, speaker, earpiece and call routes to the surya UCM profile.

The profile defined the speaker but WITHOUT a power-on sequence, and did not
define capture at all. Consequences:

- The two tas2562 amplifiers come up with the volume at zero, so playback could
  not be heard even though the stream was running. A script had to be run by
  hand (audio-salida altavoz); now the profile itself does it.
- PipeWire and PulseAudio did not expose any capture source, so no application
  could record even though the kernel did capture through raw ALSA.

The microphone sequence comes from mixer_paths_wcd9375.xml in the surya device
tree in LineageOS, where "handset-mic" uses ADC1 with TX SMIC MUX0 = ADC0.

Verified by emitting a pulsed 997 Hz tone and recording with parecord: the
energy in that bin rises up to 5100 times above its neighbours and follows the
emitted pattern exactly.

Details that have to be respected:

- The capture frontend has to be MultiMedia3. MultiMedia1 is bidirectional and,
  when recording through it, q6asm also tries to open a write stream and fails.
- The DMICn Switch controls have to stay off: turned on, they put a second
  SoundWire port into the same stream and degrade capture.
- The amplifier prefixes are the other way round from what the device tree
  suggests: "Left" is the bottom speaker and "Right" the top earpiece.
- Never set 110 as the amplifier volume: on this kernel the maximum writes a
  zero coefficient and leaves the amplifier mute.

--- a/ucm2/Xiaomi/surya/HiFi.conf	2026-05-13 01:14:07.000000000 +0200
+++ b/ucm2/Xiaomi/surya/HiFi.conf	2026-08-10 18:48:15.485526588 +0200
@@ -5,9 +5,19 @@
 	EnableSequence [
 		# SPEAKER AND EARPIECE PLAYBACK
 		cset "name='TERT_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1' 1"
+
+		# In-call audio. The DSP bridges the modem with these two
+		# backends when q6voiced opens the voice session; while there
+		# is no call they do nothing, so they can be left in place.
+		#   out: the voice goes out through TERT_MI2S_RX to the tas2562
+		#   in:  the microphone comes in through TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3
+		cset "name='TERT_MI2S_RX Voice Mixer VoiceMMode1' 1"
+		cset "name='VoiceMMode1 Capture Mixer TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3' 1"
 	]
 
 	DisableSequence [
+		cset "name='VoiceMMode1 Capture Mixer TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3' 0"
+		cset "name='TERT_MI2S_RX Voice Mixer VoiceMMode1' 0"
 		cset "name='TERT_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1' 0"
 	]
 
@@ -16,12 +26,104 @@
 	}
 }
 
+# The tas2562 volume is NOT touched from here, on purpose.
+#
+# The ALSA control "Digital Volume Control" is no use on this kernel: it writes
+# a coefficient into the chip that does not match the value asked for. Measured
+# on 2026-08-10, setting 100 from the UCM left the hardware at 0x197a967f
+# (about -14 dB), which cannot be heard. Worse: that write overwrote the good
+# value the audio-volume service had already set over I2C at boot.
+#
+# It is governed by audio-output/audio-volume, which write over I2C in the order
+# the chip expects. All that is left here is the routing.
+#
+# Two things to watch out for, verified by ear on 2026-08-05:
+#   - The prefixes are the reverse of what the device tree suggests:
+#     "Left" (9-004d) is the bottom SPEAKER and "Right" (9-004c) the EARPIECE.
+#   - NEVER set 110: on this kernel the maximum writes a zero coefficient and
+#     leaves the amplifier mute. The usable ceiling is 109.
+# The value is set to 0 before the good one because the driver caches and does
+# not rewrite when it matches what it already holds.
+
 SectionDevice."Speaker" {
 	Comment "Speaker playback"
 
+	# Both share the same PCM and only one can play. Without declaring it,
+	# the audio server keeps just one and the other does not show up as a
+	# selectable port.
+	ConflictingDevice [
+		"Earpiece"
+	]
+
 	Value {
 		PlaybackPriority 200
 		PlaybackPCM "hw:${CardId},0"
 		PlaybackChannels 1
 	}
 }
+
+SectionDevice."Earpiece" {
+	Comment "Earpiece playback"
+
+	ConflictingDevice [
+		"Speaker"
+	]
+
+	Value {
+		PlaybackPriority 100
+		PlaybackPCM "hw:${CardId},0"
+		PlaybackChannels 1
+	}
+}
+
+SectionDevice."Mic" {
+	Comment "Bottom microphone"
+
+	EnableSequence [
+		# The capture frontend is MultiMedia3; MultiMedia1 is
+		# bidirectional, and recording through it makes q6asm also try
+		# to open a write stream, and fail.
+		cset "name='MultiMedia3 Mixer TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3' 1"
+
+		# Decimator 0 <- SoundWire, channel ADC0 (= the codec's ADC1,
+		# the main microphone according to Xiaomi's mixer_paths).
+		cset "name='TX_AIF1_CAP Mixer DEC0' 1"
+		cset "name='TX DEC0 MUX' SWR_MIC"
+		cset "name='TX SMIC MUX0' ADC0"
+
+		# The DMICn Switches have to stay off: switched on they put a
+		# second SoundWire port into the same stream and degrade the
+		# capture.
+		cset "name='DMIC0 Switch' 0"
+		cset "name='DMIC1 Switch' 0"
+		cset "name='DMIC2 Switch' 0"
+		cset "name='DMIC3 Switch' 0"
+
+		# Analogue gain at maximum (better signal-to-noise than raising
+		# the digital one) and the digital one just enough for the
+		# recording to be audible: 94 is +10 dB on the control's scale,
+		# which runs -84 dB to +40 dB in 1 dB steps.
+		cset "name='ADC1 Switch' 1"
+		cset "name='ADC1_MIXER Switch' 1"
+		cset "name='ADC1 Volume' 20"
+		cset "name='TX_DEC0 Volume' 104"
+	]
+
+	DisableSequence [
+		cset "name='ADC1_MIXER Switch' 0"
+		cset "name='ADC1 Switch' 0"
+		cset "name='TX SMIC MUX0' ZERO"
+		cset "name='TX_AIF1_CAP Mixer DEC0' 0"
+		cset "name='MultiMedia3 Mixer TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3' 0"
+	]
+
+	Value {
+		CapturePriority 200
+		# CAREFUL: the PCM number depends on the ORDER of the links in the
+		# device tree. Adding the codec playback link (the headphones) moved
+		# MultiMedia3 from 2 to 3 and VoiceMMode1 from 4 to 5, and the card
+		# stopped opening (sink auto_null). Check with /proc/asound/pcm.
+		CapturePCM "hw:${CardId},3"
+		CaptureChannels 1
+	}
+}
